- dtwm, DtSearch, dtsr: five signal handlers did Xlib, Xt, stdio or exit work
  inside the signal. The termination handler of dtwm (INT, HUP, QUIT, TERM) now
  calls only `XtNoticeSignal()`; the confirmation or the shutdown runs from an
  `XtAppAddSignal()` procedure that is registered right after the application
  context is created, a second signal and the time before that exit with
  `_exit()`. The handler of the fatal signals (ILL, FPE, BUS, SEGV, SYS) keeps
  its best-effort reset of the keyboard focus, which cannot be deferred, and is
  now installed with `SA_RESETHAND | SA_NODEFER` and leaves with `_exit()` when
  entered twice. `signal_abort()` of libDtSearch (installed with `DtSrInSIGNAL`)
  wrote the message list and a catalog message with stdio and ran the exit
  callbacks; the library has no event loop and no regularly reached safe point,
  so it now writes one fixed line with `write()` and calls `_exit(100 + sig)`;
  the exit callbacks (database close) no longer run, which matters only for a
  client that opens the databases for writing. `dtsrdelete` (tomita.c) installed
  `DtSearchExit()` and a handler that called `printf()`; the handler now only
  sets a `volatile sig_atomic_t`, INT and TERM interrupt the pending read, and
  the engine shutdown, the exit callbacks and the message run from the main flow
  at the next check (a second signal calls `_exit()`). The shutdown flag of
  `dtsrhan.c` is a `volatile sig_atomic_t`. Tests:
