2026-11-13
- dtwm: the session file reader ignored `HOME`, and crashed when it was unset:
  the test `(home_dir = getenv("HOME")) != NULL || strlen(home_dir) == 0` was
  inverted, so it asked the password database whenever `HOME` was set and read
  a null pointer when it was not. It uses `HOME` when it is set and non-empty,
  asks the password database otherwise, and gives up if that has no home
  directory either. The counts at the top of the session file were not
  checked: a damaged file spun the reader on end of file, or made it request
  gigabytes. A count must now be readable and not above 10,000, and each
  record loop stops at the first record that cannot be read. Found by the
  `dtwm-session` fuzz target. Test: `programs/dtwm/tests/check-session-counts.py`.
- dtlogin: the port of a forwarded XDMCP request was computed over an array of
  any length in a signed `int`, which overflows. It is computed from its first
  two bytes in an unsigned type. Found by the `dtlogin-xdmcp` fuzz target.
  Test: `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-forward-port.py`.
- dtsession: reading a palette file that has too few colors for the display
  leaked the palette and its name, and called `unlink()` on the directory of
  the file. It frees the palette and no longer calls `unlink()`; the palette
  file stays, because it can suit another display (the warning text that says
  the file is removed describes what the code never did).

2026-11-13
- Fuzzing: `cde/fuzz` holds libFuzzer targets for six parsers: the dtspcd
  protocol requests (`spc-proto.c`), the XDMCP request handlers of dtlogin, the
  session file readers of dtwm and dtsession, the palette reader of dtsession
  and the property readers of dtwm. Each target extracts the production parser
  from its source file and builds it with the sanitizers, as the unit tests do;
  `fuzz/run-fuzz.py --target NAME --seconds N` runs one. See `fuzz/README`.

2026-11-13
- dtterm: two signal handlers did their cleanup inside the signal. The handler
  of the dtterm server called `XtAppAddTimeOut()`, and the handler of the
  terminal (a setuid program) ran the pty, utmp and log file cleanups with
  their locks and stdio and called `exit()`. Both now hand the work to the
  toolkit: `XtAppAddSignal()` registers the cleanup, the handler calls only
  `XtNoticeSignal()`, and a second signal exits at once with `_exit()`. The
  terminal keeps the old sequence for the short time before the application
  context exists. Tests: `programs/dtterm/tests/check-server-cleanup-signal.py`
  and `check-main-cleanup-signal.py`.

2026-11-13
- dtspcd: the SIGCHLD handler of the daemon reaped the children itself. It took
  the process lock, reported errors and called the termination handler of the
  channel from the signal, which can deadlock the daemon when the signal
  interrupts a thread of control that holds the lock or the log. The handler
  now writes one byte to a non-blocking, close-on-exec pipe that the event loop
  waits on, and the loop reaps the children (`SPC_Child_Pipe_Open()`,
  `SPC_Reap_Children()` in `lib/DtSvc/DtEncap/spc-exec.c`). A daemon must
  install its event loop hooks before it opens a channel; the request test
  does so now. The handlers of both pipes read the result of `write()` without
  using it, as a full pipe already holds a wake-up. Test:
  `lib/DtSvc/DtEncap/tests/check-spc-child-signal.py`.

2026-11-13
- dtspcd: the exit timer ran in the SIGALRM handler. The handler took the log
  lock, wrote to the log with stdio, closed the log and called `exit()`, from
  a signal that can arrive while the daemon is in the middle of any of those,
  which can deadlock the daemon or corrupt its output. The handler now only
  writes one byte to a pipe, and the daemon's event loop waits on the other
  end and runs the former handler body outside signal context. The pipe is
  non-blocking and close-on-exec, so no sub-process inherits it. Test:
  `programs/dtspcd/tests/check-exit-timer-signal.py`.

2026-11-13
- Signals: 132 calls of `signal()` in 26 C sources use `_DtSignal()`, a
  header-only wrapper over `sigaction()` in the new `Dt/DtSigP.h`. It keeps the
  behaviour of the `signal()` of glibc and the BSD systems (the handler stays
  installed, interrupted calls restart, no other signal is blocked), which the
  C standard leaves open for `signal()`. The calls in files that tests name are
  not converted yet.

2026-11-13
- Allocation sizes: 545 allocations of the form `n * sizeof(T)` in 187 C
  sources no longer multiply unchecked. `malloc()` and `XtMalloc()` become
  `calloc()` and `XtCalloc()`, and `realloc()` becomes `reallocarray()`, which
  fail (or, for the Xt calls, report an error) when the product overflows
  instead of allocating a short block that the caller then fills. The
  allocations are now zero-filled. Files that the failure-injection tests
  extract keep their `malloc()` and `realloc()` calls so the tests still replace
  them, and the 158 `XtRealloc()` products have no array form in Xt and are
  still to be done.

2026-11-13
- C23 null pointer constant: the 1,100 non-generated C sources use `nullptr`
  in place of `NULL` (37,000 uses). Headers keep `NULL` because C++ and
  pre-C23 consumers include them. `NULL` stays where C23 does not allow
  `nullptr`: in `XtSetArg()` and `objxm_obj_set_ui_arg()` arguments (they
  convert the value to an integer) and in casts to non-pointer types such as
  `(XtArgVal) NULL`. The test runners that compile or match production text
  use `-std=gnu23` and read the source with `nullptr` read back as `NULL`, so
  they still match the older revisions of the negative controls. A test fixture
  named `unreachable` was renamed because C23 defines a macro of that name.

2026-11-13
- Legacy C syntax: the BSD calls `bcopy()`, `bzero()`, `bcmp()` and `index()` of
  the C sources are replaced by `memmove()`, `memset()`, `memcmp()` and
  `strchr()`, and the compatibility declarations and macros for them are
  removed (`include/bms/sbstdinc.h`, `include/Dt/DtosP.h`, the dtlogin and
  dtcreate headers). The storage class `register` is removed from the C code.
  109 K&R function definitions and about 170 declarations with empty
  parentheses, which C23 reads as `(void)`, now carry full prototypes
  (`lib/tt` demos, dtcm, dtudcfonted, dtwm, dtlogin, the examples and others);
  parameters declared `char`, `short` or `float` keep their promoted types. The
  programs that declared `void main` return `int`. The unbuilt sources of
  dtudcfonted and the code under `#if 0` were converted with the rest but are
  not compiled by this tree.

2026-11-13
- tt, dtinfo, dtmail and the drag and drop example: seven more places made a
  temporary name with `tempnam()` or `mktemp()` and used it later. They now
  create the file with `mkstemp()` (mode 0600) or, where the name stands for a
  directory (`tttar`) or holds a FIFO (`tttrace`), the directory with
  `mkdtemp()` (mode 0700). A failed creation is reported instead of using the
  predictable name; `tttar` now exits with an error where it used to fail later
  at `rmdir`, and the saved session of dtmail is no longer stored under a
  predictable `session.N` name. Tests plant a link at the old name and fail on
  the previous code: `lib/tt/bin/tests`, `programs/dtmail/tests`,
  `programs/dtinfo/dtinfo/src/UAS/DtSR/tests` and `examples/dtdnd/tests`.

2026-11-12
- DtWidget, DtHelp, dtcm, dtstyle, dticon, dtpad, dtcreate and dtudcfonted:
  twelve more places made a temporary file name with `tmpnam()`, `tempnam()`
  or `mktemp()` and opened it later. They now create the file with `mkstemp()`
  (mode 0600) and report a failed creation instead of using the predictable
  name. The uncompress and interpreter temporary files of DtHelp
  (`Access.c`, `CanvasOs.c`), the editor files of DtWidget, the palette file
  of dtstyle (`mkstemps()`, the name carries the palette suffix), the start
  file of dticon, the editor file of dtpad and the icon files of dtcreate are
  covered; dtpad no longer falls back to a predictable name under `/usr/tmp`.
  The predictable-name block under `#ifdef NOT` in `dtcm/dnd.c` was removed.
  Tests plant a symbolic link at the old name and fail on the previous code.
- DtHelp: the interpreter buffer in `CanvasOs.c` was allocated with
  `sizeof(interp)` of a pointer, which a long name overflowed; it is sized from
  the string now.
- DtSvc, dtwm and dtpdmd: five places made a temporary file name with
  `tempnam()`, `mktemp()` or `tmpnam()` and opened it later, so a local user
  could plant a file or symbolic link at the name first. The logfile of the
  noio channel (`DtEncap/noio.c`), the log file of `spc-error.c`, the session
  save path (`DtUtil1/SmUtil.c`), the cpp output of the dtwm configuration
  (`WmResParse.c`) and the authorization file of dtpdmd (`manager.c`) now create
  the file with `mkstemp()` (mode 0600), and a failed creation is reported
  instead of falling back to the predictable name. Tests:
  `check-spc-logfile-create.py`, `check-spc-log-create.py`,
  `check-session-save-path.py`, `check-config-temp-file.py`,
  `check-auth-file.py`, each of which plants a link at the old name.
- DtHelp and DtTerm: twenty-two libc `realloc()` calls stored their result in
  the pointer they grew, so a failed growth leaked the block and the next use
  wrote through NULL. They now grow through a temporary and keep the old block
  (`CanvasOs.c`, `LinkMgr.c`, `UtilSDL.c`, `FormatSDL.c`, `Font.c`,
  `AccessSDL.c`, `StringFuncs.c`, `CvString.c` in DtHelp; `lineToData.c`,
  `TermPrimDebug.c` and `TermPrimGetPty-bsd.c` in DtTerm), with the size
  multiplications checked. `CreateAsciiString()` now returns whether the
  growth succeeded. The growth in `lineToData.c` ends the program with a
  message, as the other allocation failures of that tool do.
- DtHelp: `_DtHelpCeIconvStr()` called `iconv()` with a NULL output buffer when
  the caller gave no buffer, which aborts inside current glibc. The first
  output block is now allocated before the conversion. Tests:
  `lib/DtHelp/tests/check-realloc-failures.py` (linked against the built
  libraries) and `lib/DtTerm/tests/check-realloc-failures.py`.
- DtSvc: eight growths of libc `realloc()` lists in place of their own pointer
  leaked the list and then wrote through NULL when the allocation failed:
  the pid list of `spc-exec.c`, the logfile list of `noio.c`, the file and name
  lists of `MMDb.c`, and the entry table of `addToRes.c`. Each growth now goes
  through a temporary, keeps the old list when it fails, and checks its size
  multiplication for overflow. A child's pid slot is reserved before `fork()`
  (value -2 until the pid is stored; a failed fork records -1, the dead value).
  Tests: `DtEncap/tests/check-spc-list-alloc.py`, `tests/check-mmdb-alloc.py`,
  `tests/check-addtores-alloc.py`. The two `_DtAppendToBuffer` sites are
  unchanged: that function cannot report a failure.
- dtspcd and DtEncap: the environment file paths are allocated for each path
  and checked (`tests/check-env-file-alloc.py`, `DtEncap/tests/check-spc-env-alloc.py`).
- DtSvc and dtspcd: the default environment files were built with `realloc()`
  of a pointer whose result was not checked and was then written through, and
  the first buffer was not checked either (`lib/DtSvc/DtEncap/spc-env.c`,
  `programs/dtspcd/main.c`). Each buffer is now allocated for its own path and
  skipped if the allocation fails. Expanding a variable reference in a
  subprocess environment kept the result of a failed `realloc()` as the new
  string, so a failure wrote through NULL; a failed `realloc()` now keeps the
  unexpanded value (`lib/DtSvc/DtEncap/spc-exec.c`, tested by
  `lib/DtSvc/DtEncap/tests/check-spc-exec-realloc.py`).
- dtstyle: an unset `HOME` was dereferenced; it is now treated as empty
  (`programs/dtstyle/Main.c`).
- dtwm: the panel and subpanel session file readers stored a word of any length
  in 256-byte buffers; the words are now read with a width of 255
  (`programs/dtwm/Session.c`).
- dtcalc: the temporary `.dtcalcrc` name came from `mktemp()` and was then
  opened separately; it is now created with `mkstemp()` and opened from its
  descriptor (`programs/dtcalc/calctool.c`).
- dtpad: the Save As dialog built its starting directory in a 1024-byte buffer
  and copied text into it without a length check. A file name typed with a
  directory longer than that buffer, or a directory that the dialog reports
  long, overflowed the stack, and the directory mask could overflow the same
  buffer. The text read from `XmStringGetLtoR()` was written to a pointer that
  the call then replaced, so its buffer leaked, and the call's result was not
  checked. The directory is now stored only when it fits with the mask
  (`programs/dtpad/dirselect.c`, which uses no Motif or Xt type and is tested
  under the sanitizers by `tests/check-dirselect.py`). A directory that does not
  fit is replaced by the current directory of the process, and a dialog that
  reports no directory now starts in that directory too, not in a relative one.
  The fault was a stack overflow that a user could trigger by typing a long
  name, so it is in the defect list of the security-first audit.

2026-11-11
- libtt, dtcm: review of what a program that gives the library a shell widget
  is answered for without asking (the window requests, `Quit`, `Pause`,
  `Resume`): they act on the shell window only and stay; the reasons are in
  `docs/improvements/tooltalk.md`. The review found two faults. `Quit` read its
  force argument from the first argument, so a `Quit` with only the force
  argument closed the shell of a program that had no callback instead of
  failing with `TT_DESKTOP_ENOTSUP`, and the `WM_DELETE_WINDOW` event it sends
  is now built whole (it was sent with fields never set) and its dead second
  check of the widget is gone. `dtcm` and its appointment editor passed the
  client data of the contract to `ttdt_message_accept()` as the shell and the frame
  as the client data, so the window requests of the contract (`Get_Geometry`,
  `Raise`, `Pause` and so on) ran on a calendar record as if it were a widget; they
  pass the frame as the shell. Covered by a lifecycle case of the session requests
  test, which runs `Quit` with the silent argument, the force argument and neither,
  and `Pause` and `Resume` twice, on a process with a shell and no callback.
- libtt: `Set_XInfo` is answered only by a program that asks for it. A program
  that gave `ttdt_session_join()` or `ttdt_message_accept()` a shell widget had a
  handler registered for it, which set `$DISPLAY` from the message of any program of
  the session; the handler read the display through the wrong type, so the variable
  became a few unrelated bytes of the stack. The request is now registered only
  with `TTDT_SESSION_SET_XINFO` (a new bit of `Ttdt_session_requests`, in `dtksh`
  `ttdt_session_join ... TTDT_SET_XINFO`) or, for a contract, with the new last
  argument of `ttdt_message_accept()`, a `Ttdt_contract_requests` that is zero or
  `TTDT_CONTRACT_SET_XINFO` (every caller passes `TTDT_CONTRACT_NO_REQUESTS`; any
  other value is `TT_ERR_NUM` and accepts nothing). Without a shell the request
  needs a callback (`TT_ERR_POINTER`). Otherwise it fails like any request nobody
  answers (`TT_ERR_NO_MATCH`, or `TT_DESKTOP_ENOTSUP` when addressed to the
  program). When it is answered with a shell it does what its handler always
  meant to: it sets `$DISPLAY`, and leaves the windows and the connection of the
  program where they are. The display must be of the form host or socket, colon and
  number (at most 512 characters, no null byte), the visual one of the six names of
  `X.h` and the depth 1 to 32, else the request fails and nothing is set
  (`TT_DESKTOP_EINVAL`, `_EPROTO` for a missing display or a null byte). The
  request built by `ttdt_Set_XInfo()` sent its depth in-out, which the pattern of a
  handler (in) never matched; it is in now. The sizes of the strings of the request
  are checked before any callback sees them. The manual pages and guides (English
  and Japanese), the `dtksh` documents and the documented-calls check follow, and
  the check now also requires the description of the `dtksh` command to name every
  request it takes. Covered by the session requests test of
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check` (with an Xvfb server for the cases that have
  a shell; against the previous library its checks of `Set_XInfo` fail); described in
  `docs/improvements/tooltalk.md`.
- dtmail, dtmailpr: remove the message "The dtmail program is not running as
  group \"mail\"" and the texts around it, which nothing has shown since the
  mailer stopped needing the mail group. `DTME_BadRunGroup` is gone from
  `DTMailError_t`, from the fall-back texts of `libDtMail` and from set 1 of
  `libDtMail.msg`, and message 4 ("Mailer has not been properly installed,
  and cannot run because the execution group is incorrectly set") is gone from
  set 2 of `DtMail.msg`; the two help sections of the Mailer volume that
  explained them, *Mailer has not been properly installed* and *Exit Mail?*,
  are gone from the volume of every locale. The catalogs are indexed by number
  (`libDtMail` looks an error up as message "enumerator + 1", `dtmail` names
  messages of `DtMail.msg` in its `CATGETS` calls), so every message after a
  removed one has a new number: the English sources, the sources of the
  locales and the 31 calls that named a message of set 2 were renumbered
  together, with no entry left in the place of the old one. A catalog that
  was installed before this change shows the wrong text under many errors
  and mailer messages until it is installed again. A new check,
  `libDtMail/tests/check-message-catalogs.py` (run by `make check`), reads
  every catalog source against the enumeration, the fall-back texts and the
  calls (same numbers, same error named above each message, same conversions,
  the installed locales with exactly the English set of messages), runs
  gencat on every source and reads each compiled catalog back, and looks every
  error up through the production `DtMailEnv` under the sanitizers with the
  English catalog, with each installed locale and without a catalog; it fails
  on the tree before this change and on eighteen deliberately broken copies
  of the catalogs, the code and the help volumes.
- Documentation: every manual page, guide, example, demo and message catalog
  shows the calls of the Desktop interface the way `include/Tt/tttk.h` declares
  them. The Japanese guides (`ttGuide`, the programmer's overview and the guide to
  `dtksh`) still showed `ttdt_session_join()` with five arguments and
  `ttdt_sender_imprint_on()` with nine; they now show the `requests` and
